    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 042007\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion \nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV \n107 PM PDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry and breezy conditions will persist into Saturday.\n\n* Rain and storm chances return Saturday afternoon and Sunday as \n  moisture surges northward\n\n* Drier conditions for Labor Day, monsoonal moisture and warmer \n  temperatures may return mid next week. \n  \n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Mid Next Week.\n\nDry and breezy conditions across the much of the region today as \nupper troughing persists across the western US. Dew points are \ncurrently in the 30s and 40s across areas west of the Colorado River \nwhile areas across northwest Arizona currently sit in the 50s to \nlower 60s. This will generally translate into a few clouds over \nMohave County, but with weak instability and overall shallow \nmoisture anything other than a few virga showers are not expected.\nBreezy conditions will continue across much of the region this \nafternoon and Saturday with winds generally gusting between 30-35 \nmph, although a few higher elevations and wind prone valleys seeing \ngusts around 40 mph. Due to the lower wind threshold on area lakes, \na Lake Wind Advisory remains in effect into this evening for Lake \nMead and Lake Mohave. \n\nExpect some significant changes this weekend as tropical moisture \nfrom Hurricane Marie is pulled north by the aforementioned west \ncoast trough. We will start to see PWATs increase as early as \nSaturday morning with the 1.00\" line expanding into the lower \nColorado River Valley by late Saturday morning and expanding into \nClark, San Bernardino, and Mohave counties by Sunday morning. Even \ndeeper moisture is expected to advect into the region by Sunday \nafternoon with PWATs over the southern CWA reaching around 1.50\". \nSome isolated showers will be possible as early as Saturday over \nsouthern Mohave County due to a weak shortwave tracking northward. \nInstability remains rather weak with CAPE values generally less than \n200 J/kg, but the shortwave itself may be enough to generate a few \nshowers in the afternoon. Confidence is increasing that portions of \nthe area will see a band of light to moderate rainfall Sunday \nextending from the southwest to the northeast. Exactly where this \nband sets up will be a big determining factor on how much and who \nhas the highest threat, but at this point the models have been \nfavoring areas generally south and east of a line extending from \nCaliente to Desert Rock to Ridgecrest. This area is also in a \nfavored right entrance region of a 100+ knot jet which should also \nhelp to support lift and combined with the previously mentioned mid-\nupper shortwave moving north will provide enough lift to enhance an \narea of showers and isolated convection. Rainfall amounts are \ngenerally expected to be around 0.10\"-0.50\", but a lot will depend \non where this band sets up. Temperatures on Sunday will be rather \ncool and likely around 10 degrees below normal; however, for areas \nthat see clouds and rain could potentially see highs 15-20 degrees \nbelow normal. \n\nLooking toward next week, longer range guidance shows drier air \nfiltering back into the region Labor Day and Tuesday. A \nSouthwesterly flow pattern looks to somewhat remain in place next \nweek, however guidance continues to hint at the upper jet retreating \nnorthward as ridging tries to rebuild from New Mexico westward into \nthe Mojave Desert. While Monday and Tuesday look much drier than \nSunday, watching for signals that a monsoonal pattern may be trying \nto set up again going into mid to late next week. Lower confidence \nexists in the overall details, but confidence is slowly increasing \nin a pattern supportive of monsoon activity returning, which is also \nreflected in CPC's 6-10 day precipitation outlook. In fact, CPC \nhighlights portions of the CRV and eastern Mojave Desert for slight \nrisk of heavy precipitation Sept 11-13th. CPC 6-10 day outlook now \nshows a signal toward a pattern supportive of above normal \ntemperatures locally as ridging builds over the Southwest.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...South-\nsouthwest winds continue throughout the TAF period, strongest during \nthe daytime hours with gusts 20-30 knots likely (90%). Probability \nof seeing 35+ knots is ~10%. Clear skies with temperatures remaining \nbelow 100F. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty conditions resume \ntoday with southerly to westerly gusts across the region of 20-30 \nknots. Breezes weaken after sunset, but remain elevated in most \nspots overnight. Mostly clear skies with just a few high clouds \nmoving into our southern areas on Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow/Moore \nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
